# Character consistency

## Purpose

Prevent identity drift and identity substitution. The same character MUST
read as the same person in every panel of every page, and no character may be
drawn with another one's face.

## Scope

Visual layer. Applies to character design, reference sheets, reference plans,
prompt compilation, and every render that depicts a `CHAR_*` entity.

## Core Principles

- Identity lives in approved masters. Text descriptions supplement the
  reference images and NEVER replace them.
- A name does not carry a face. Writing "Oskar" in a prompt or a balloon does
  not stop a model from drawing Mara twice.
- Drift compounds. Never draw a character from a previous page alone. Anchor
  every request to the master.
- Characters who could be confused are designed apart on purpose, and the
  difference is stated in words on every request that shows both.

## Identity anchors

- Every character file lists `distinguishing_features`: what separates this
  character from anyone else in the cast. At least one, and they SHOULD be
  visible at small panel sizes. Mara: broken nose, cropped grey hair, an
  oilskin coat torn at the left cuff.
- `description` records age, build, hair, face, and skin as separate fields.
  `prompt_summary` compresses them into one literal sentence for prompts.
- Anchors survive stylization. When a sheet simplifies shapes for the
  drawing style, the anchors stay readable. A grayscale photograph is a
  reference for a face. It is not a drawing in the comic's style and is not
  attached as one.
- Asymmetric anchors (a torn left cuff, a cut on the left hand) are recorded
  with their anatomical side and checked that way.

## Master references

- Every character who appears on more than one page MUST have an approved
  identity reference before any of those pages is generated. For the main
  cast that is a turnaround (`CHAR_<NAME>_TURN_MASTER_V##`) and, where the
  bible asks for them, expression and wardrobe sheets.
- Recurring secondary characters get references too. A turnaround is the
  minimum for anyone the reader must recognize across panels.
- Sheets are drawn in the comic's own style, with readable faces, plain
  backgrounds, and consistent scale. Dramatic lighting is for the pages.
- Every request that depicts a character attaches that character's current
  identity master in the `identity` role. A style reference never stands in
  for it (`image/reference-roles.md`).
- When the provider's reference limit forces a choice, the look master and
  the identity masters of the cast on the page come first. Wardrobe comes
  next, environment after that. If the cast of a page does not fit the
  limit, change the page plan or generate per panel. Do not drop an identity
  reference.
- Redesigns supersede masters with a new `_V##`
  (`common/approval-policy.md`). Mixing versions of one character within a
  scene is forbidden.

## Visually adjacent characters

- Two characters are adjacent when a reader, or a model, could take one for
  the other: similar age and build, the same uniform, the same hair.
- Adjacent characters list each other in `visually_adjacent_to`. The link
  SHOULD be declared in both files.
- Adjacent characters MUST be given distinct face shapes, hair, facial hair
  (where either has any), builds, and clothing silhouettes. Where two designs
  share one of these, one design changes before pages are generated.
- Every prompt for a page that shows two adjacent characters states the
  differences in words, in addition to attaching both identity masters:
  "Oskar: round face, hooded jacket. Mara: long face, cropped grey hair,
  oilskin coat."
- An identity failure between adjacent characters is fixed upstream first:
  sharpen the bible and the sheets, then repair the page
  (`common/repair-limits.md`).

## Identity checks

- After each generation, compare every drawn character with the identity
  master: facial structure, hair, facial hair, distinguishing marks, apparent
  age, build, costume. A mismatch on any of these is a finding, however good
  the page looks otherwise.
- Check per character and per panel, and count each identity separately. Two
  Maras in one panel, or a missing Oskar, fails `cast_count`.
- Approved masters outrank bible text. New art is checked against the
  approved image first and the written description second, including the
  extent of marks (how far a tear runs, how large a scar is). Where image
  and text disagree, the bible is stale. Sync it before generating anything
  that would inherit the difference.
- Hair, facial hair, and marks do not change within a scene unless the script
  says so.
- One substituted or ambiguous face on a page is a blocking finding for the
  whole page.

## Validation

- `character.schema.json` requires `description.summary`, at least one entry
  in `distinguishing_features`, and the `references` list.
- `scripts/validate-workspace.js` schema-checks every character and wardrobe
  file and checks that its ID matches its directory. An asset ID is resolved
  against the registry where a page, a reference plan, or a page prompt
  package cites it. `scripts/check-continuity.js` reports a ledger row that
  names a character or wardrobe state the workspace does not define.
- `hooks/protect-approved-assets.js` blocks writes to approved masters.
- No script can judge whether two designs are far enough apart, or whether a
  character who recurs has a reference that is good enough. The
  character-designer and the art-director decide at the visual development
  gate, and `production-qa` checks that every recurring character lists an
  approved reference before pages are generated.
- Likeness is visual review: the `identity` and `cast_count` checks of every
  review compare the pixels with the master sheet, and only a person or a
  reviewing agent looking at the render can fill them in.
